WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:17.000
Who can tell what the future will be like?
NASA scientists combined several climate models
to show projected temperature and precipitation
patterns through the end of the 21st century.

00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:35.000
The visualization compares projected changes
with late 20th-century average levels.
The largest temperature increases are in
the Northern Hemisphere.

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:54.000
Across North America, snowy areas such as
the Rocky Mountains and northern Canada will
see some of the earliest and largest rises.

00:00:54.000 --> 00:01:14.000
Warming reduces the reflective cooling effect
of snow cover. As Arctic sea ice melts,
darker ocean water absorbs more solar energy.

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:31.000
The warmer water then accelerates sea-ice melt.
Land areas generally warm more than nearby oceans.

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:49.000
Ocean evaporation helps keep the surface cool,
and the deep ocean can absorb substantial energy
before its temperature rises.

00:01:49.000 --> 00:02:06.000
Off South America, a warm patch of the Pacific
is associated with El Niño, a recurring cycle
that brings more precipitation to the region.

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:23.000
Models project warmer Pacific waters and
increased precipitation toward Indonesia
as the century progresses.

00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:39.000
Monsoons may bring more precipitation across
a wider area of the Indian subcontinent.

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:55.000
Eastern Africa and the Sahel may see increases
up to 100 percent; the Mediterranean may see
a decrease greater than 40 percent.

00:02:55.000 --> 00:03:13.000
Dry mid-latitude and subtropical regions
are projected to receive less precipitation,
while wetter mid-latitudes may receive more.

00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:30.000
The poles and high latitudes are also expected
to see more precipitation.

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:52.900
In North America, models indicate more
precipitation in the north and less in the south.
